          "status": "回复已成稿",
          "pending": false,
          "syncKey": "block:general-editing",
          "syncMarkdown": "We value your suggestion to examine the category-level results more closely and explore why some categories decline despite broadly maintained overall general editing performance. Table 6 reports an overall ImgEdit-Bench score of 4.39 for PhysFlow and 4.41 for Qwen-Image-Edit-2511. PhysFlow scores higher in six of nine categories, while the largest declines occur in Hybrid (3.76 to 3.26) and Extract (4.16 to 3.91).\n\nWe attribute the declines in Hybrid and Extract to task-dependent transfer from physical-process supervision. PhysTrace emphasizes causally connected state evolution within a physical process through textual rationales and intermediate visual states (Sections 3.2 to 3.4). Hybrid combines separately specified editing operations within one instruction, whereas Extract requires isolating a subject while preserving its identity and visual details. This difference in task focus may help explain why improvements in physical-process editing do not transfer uniformly to Hybrid and Extract.\n\nWe have revised Appendix C.1 to report these category-level declines alongside the improvements. The revised text describes broadly maintained overall performance while explicitly acknowledging the lower scores in Hybrid and Extract.",
          "markdown": "We value your suggestion to examine the category-level results more closely and explore why some categories decline despite broadly maintained overall general editing performance. Table 6 reports an overall ImgEdit-Bench score of 4.39 for PhysFlow and 4.41 for Qwen-Image-Edit-2511. PhysFlow scores higher in six of nine categories, while the largest declines occur in Hybrid (3.76 to 3.26) and Extract (4.16 to 3.91).\n\nWe attribute the declines in Hybrid and Extract to task-dependent transfer from physical-process supervision. PhysTrace emphasizes causally connected state evolution within a physical process through textual rationales and intermediate visual states (Sections 3.2 to 3.4). Hybrid combines separately specified editing operations within one instruction, whereas Extract requires isolating a subject while preserving its identity and visual details. This difference in task focus may help explain why improvements in physical-process editing do not transfer uniformly to Hybrid and Extract.\n\nWe have revised Appendix C.1 to report these category-level declines alongside the improvements. The revised text describes broadly maintained overall performance while explicitly acknowledging the lower scores in Hybrid and Extract.",
